{"author":{"id":"609bf866d4a5d716c19faf5d617b3bf3de478230d29602d72aad5aa90a80466f"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1794655183,"nonce":"fvs1ncW1XmEM4TkJ53XrNqNlTTs=","message":"Phase 0 schema drafted and committed locally as aac22d82 (crates/ash_tast), not pushed.\n\nWhat it is: fixed-size little-endian records with no alignment requirement (zerocopy's LE types), read in place from a memory-mapped file. A 32-byte header carries the magic, a layout version, a hash of the whole layout, the Haxe version and the entry function. A section table follows, then 15 sections: Strings (one UTF-8 blob), StringIndex, Positions, Types, TypeArgs, Classes, Fields, Abstracts, Enums, EnumCtors, AnonFields, Locals, Params, Funcs and Nodes. Cross-references are indexes, with u32::MAX for none. A node is 28 bytes: kind, flags, type, position, two operand words, and a child range. Nodes are written breadth-first, so a node's children are consecutive and come after it. Kinds 0..=28 are the TypedExprDef constructors in order; four pseudo-kinds (CASE, CATCH, OBJECT_FIELD, DEFAULT) hold what TypedExprDef keeps in anonymous structures. The per-kind contract (what each puts in flags, a, b and its children) is a table in format.rs.\n\nReader: `Tast::parse(\u0026[u8])` casts each section in place, then checks every reference, every string boundary, every child range, the child-after-parent rule (so a walk cannot loop) and each kind's child count, once. After it returns the accessors do not re-check.\n\nGenerator: the `record!` macro defines each struct and its descriptor together; `gen_haxe` writes `haxe/ash/tast/Format.hx` from the descriptors (constants, one writer per record, and `checkCompiler()`, which compares the TypedExprDef, Binop and Unop constructors with the running compiler). A test fails if the committed Format.hx is stale, and another pins the layout hash so a change is deliberate.\n\nChecked: 12 tests (round trip, child layout, damaged header, bad UTF-8, bad ids, bad arity, hash, generated file current); clippy clean. A one-function program written from Haxe with the generated writers is accepted by the Rust reader (`tast_dump --tree`). `checkCompiler()` passes on Haxe 4.3.6, and it caught two mistakes of mine in the operator tables (OpNullCoal, OpSpread).\n\nNot done: the exporter macro itself, the type interning on the Haxe side, the AIR lowering, and positions in the nodes the macro writes.","files":null}]}